```javascript
// Lists (Arrays in JavaScript)
// Use when:
// - You need a mutable (changeable) collection of items.
// - The order of elements matters.
// - You need to add, remove, or modify elements frequently.
// - You don't need to guarantee the size of the collection.

// Tuples (Not directly supported in JavaScript, but can be emulated)
// Use when:
// - You need an immutable (unchangeable) collection of items.
// - The order of elements matters.
// - You want to ensure data integrity and prevent accidental modification.
// - You want to use the collection as a key in a JavaScript object (since object keys must be immutable).
// - You want to return multiple values from a function in a clear and concise way.

// Emulating Tuples in JavaScript (using an array with a 'readonly' flag or a custom class)

// Example using a ReadonlyArray (requires a library like readonly-array)
// npm install readonly-array
// import { ReadonlyArray } from 'readonly-array';

// const myTuple = new ReadonlyArray([1, 2, 3]);
// myTuple[0] = 4; // Throws an error because it's readonly


// Example using a custom Tuple class:
class Tuple {
  constructor(values) {
    this.values = [...values]; // Create a copy to prevent external modification
  }

  get length() {
    return this.values.length;
  }

  // Add methods for accessing elements, etc.
  // No methods to modify the tuple's contents.
}

const myTuple = new Tuple([1, 2, 3]);
console.log(myTuple.values); // [1, 2, 3]
```

Lists are mutable, meaning you can change their contents after they're created. Tuples are immutable, meaning their contents cannot be changed after creation.

If you're designing an API and have a choice, use tuples (or an immutable array emulation) when you want to guarantee that the data returned to the client will not be modified. This can help prevent bugs and make your API more predictable.  It also allows using the data as a key in an object.  If mutability is required, use lists.

```javascript
const smtplib = require('smtplib');

async function sendEmailToMultipleRecipients(senderEmail, senderPassword, recipients, subject, body) {
  try {
    const msg = {
      from: senderEmail,
      to: recipients.join(', '), // Join recipients with comma
      subject: subject,
      text: body,
    };

    const smtp = await smtplib.createConnection('smtp.example.com', 587, {
      auth: {
        user: senderEmail,
        pass: senderPassword,
      },
      tls: true,
    });

    await smtp.sendMail(msg);
    await smtp.quit();

    console.log('Email sent successfully to multiple recipients.');
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error sending email:', error);
  }
}

// Example usage:
const senderEmail = 'your_email@example.com';
const senderPassword = 'your_password';
const recipients = ['recipient1@example.com', 'recipient2@example.com', 'recipient3@example.com'];
const subject = 'Important Announcement';
const body = 'This is the email body.';

sendEmailToMultipleRecipients(senderEmail, senderPassword, recipients, subject, body);
