```javascript
// apps/app1/src/views/HomeView.vue
<template>
  <div>
    <Header />
    <h1>Home View</h1>
    <p>Some content</p>
  </div>
</template>

<script setup lang="ts">
import Header from '@namespace/shared-ui/src/components/Header.vue';

// import stuff from '@/plugins/some-logic.ts' // This import is correct
// stuff();

// import moreStuff from '@/plugins/another-logic.ts' // This import is correct
// moreStuff();

@import '@/styles/app.scss';
</script>

<style scoped>
@import '../styles/header.scss';
</style>
```

**Explanation of the fix:**

The issue arises because the `@` alias in `apps/app1/tsconfig.json` resolves to the `src` directory of `apps/app1`. When you import `Header` from `@namespace/shared-ui/src/components/Header.vue`, TypeScript correctly resolves this path.

The problem with `import '@/plugins/another-logic.ts'` in `HomeView.vue` is that the `@` alias is only defined within the `apps/app1` project's `tsconfig.json`. It doesn't extend to the `packages/shared-ui` project. Therefore, TypeScript in `apps/app1` cannot resolve the path to `src/plugins/another-logic.ts` within the `shared-ui` package.

The fix is to directly import the module using its full path relative to the `packages/shared-ui` project's root. Since the `shared-ui` package's `tsconfig.json` also has the `@/*` alias, you can import it as `@namespace/shared-ui/src/plugins/another-logic.ts`.

However, for simplicity and to avoid potential issues with relative paths within the workspace, it's generally better to import modules from workspace packages using their package name and the module path within the package.

**Note:** The `@import` directive in SASS is a Vite-specific feature and works correctly as long as the path is relative to the current file. In this case, `'../styles/header.scss'` is correct because `Header.vue` is in the `views` directory, and `header.scss` is in the `styles` directory of the `shared-ui` package.

