response_prompt:
```javascript
// There is no direct way to change the category name of ILogger instances
// created via ILoggerProvider.CreateLogger to a constant value.
// The category name is typically derived from the type name.

// However, you can achieve a similar effect by implementing a custom ILoggerProvider
// that overrides the CreateLogger method and sets the category name manually.

import { ILoggerProvider } from 'microsoft-aspnet-core';

export class CustomLoggerProvider implements ILoggerProvider {
  private static readonly DEFAULT_CATEGORY = 'MyCustomCategory';

  public static Create(services: any) {
    return new CustomLoggerProvider();
  }

  public GetLogger<T>(type: any): ILogger {
    const loggerName = type.name; // Get the type name
    const logger = new Logger(loggerName); // Create a logger with the desired name
    return logger;
  }
}

class Logger implements ILogger {
  private readonly name: string;

  constructor(name: string) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  public LogInformation(message: string) {
    console.log(`[${this.name}] ${message}`);
  }

  // Implement other ILogger methods as needed
}
```

The provided code defines a `CustomLoggerProvider` that implements the `ILoggerProvider` interface.  The `GetLogger` method overrides the default behavior of `ILoggerProvider.CreateLogger` to create a logger instance with a custom name.  In this example, the logger name is set to a constant value (`MyCustomCategory`), but you can modify this to use any desired value.  The `Logger` class is a simple implementation of the `ILogger` interface that logs messages to the console, prepending the logger name to each log message.  This approach allows you to control the category name of each logger instance, regardless of the type name.  This addresses the obfuscation issue by providing a consistent and predictable category name.

